## 6. The Physics We Use (Simplified)

We don't just throw data at ML models. We compute **derived atmospheric quantities** that have real physical meaning:

| What We Compute | What It Tells Us |
|---|---|
| **Dew Point** (Magnus formula) | The temperature at which air becomes saturated. Must ALWAYS be ≤ actual temperature. If our computation gives dew point > temperature → something is physically impossible → sensor fault |
| **Saturation Vapor Pressure** | Maximum moisture air can hold at current temperature. Computed from Clausius-Clapeyron equation — used as a **feature**, not a rule |
| **Actual Vapor Pressure** | How much moisture is actually in the air right now |
| **Vapor Pressure Deficit (VPD)** | Gap between what air *could* hold and what it *does* hold. Combines T and RH into one meaningful metric |
| **Absolute Humidity** | Grams of water per cubic meter. Provides a temperature-normalized moisture measure that complements RH |

These become **features** fed into our ML models and **consistency checks** in the physics scoring layer. They are NOT hard rules — they produce soft scores.

