## 8. How We Evaluate — Our Metrics

### Signature Metric

> **False Positive Rate during genuine weather events**

This is what separates us from simple systems. Can we let real extreme weather pass while still catching sensor faults?

### Why NOT Just "Accuracy"

If 99.5% of readings are normal, a model that says *"everything is fine"* gets 99.5% accuracy while catching zero faults. Useless. We report:

| Metric | What It Really Means |
|---|---|
| **Precision** | "Of everything we flagged, how much was actually a fault?" |
| **Recall** | "Of all real faults, how many did we catch?" |
| **F1-Score** | Balance between precision and recall |
| **FPR (weather events)** | ★ "Did we accidentally reject genuine extreme weather?" |
| **Detection Latency** | "How fast after a fault appears do we alert?" |
| **Per-class F1** | "How well do we classify each of the 6 fault types?" |

### The Most Important Test Case

| Scenario | Correct Result |
|---|---|
| Heatwave: temperature climbs to 47°C over 6 hours with consistent humidity and pressure changes | ✅ **NOT a fault** — real weather |

If our system flags a genuine heatwave as a sensor fault, we have failed the central challenge.

