## 9. Data Strategy

We use 4 tiers of data, each serving a different purpose:

### Tier 1: Real Observations (for validation — strongest evidence)

**Source**: NOAA Integrated Surface Database (ISD)
- URL: https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/land-based-station/integrated-surface-database
- Actual hourly readings from physical weather stations, including Indian IMD stations
- Free to download
- **These are real sensor observations** — closest to what IMD AWS produces

**Also check**: https://data.gov.in for any IMD-published datasets

### Tier 2: Reanalysis Data (for training normal patterns)

**Source**: Open-Meteo Historical Weather API
- URL: https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/historical-weather-api
- Hourly temperature, humidity, pressure for any location from 1940 onwards
- Free for non-commercial use; JSON via simple API call
- **Important**: This is model-derived data (ERA5 reanalysis), not raw sensor output. Excellent for learning what "normal weather" looks like, but won't contain sensor-specific faults
- We'll download data for 8 Indian cities spanning different climate zones (Delhi, Mumbai, Chennai, Jaisalmer, Shimla, Cherrapunji, Bengaluru, Guwahati)

### Tier 3: Anomaly Injection (for evaluation metrics)

**Source**: We build this ourselves
- Take real weather data from Tier 1 or 2
- Programmatically inject each of our 6 fault types with known labels
- Ground-truth labels allow precise metric calculation
- Each fault type has multiple variations (different amplitudes, durations, recovery shapes)
- The PS explicitly says evaluation will use anomaly-injected data — so this is exactly right

### Tier 4: Live Sensor (for physical demo)

**Source**: Our ESP32 + BME280 hardware
- Real-time data from our own sensor
- Shows the complete pipeline: sensor → edge QC → server → dashboard
- Anomalies created by physically disturbing the sensor

